{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## This ticket's premise is now INVERTED by a merged fix — re-scope or close\n\nThe title asserts: 'The MCP graceful-drain contract assumes certified tool bodies run off the loop thread; they do not.' As of change 2345 (merged 25ea12c200ae, ticket dewy-rotatable-tarsier / d240, closed certified), **they do**.\n\noffload_sync_tools in src/rebar/_mcp_health.py now replaces every sync tool body with an async wrapper that runs it on an anyio worker thread, and marks the tool is_async so the SDK awaits it. wire_health applies instrument_certified_tools FIRST and offload_sync_tools SECOND — that order is load-bearing, because the instrumenter installs a sync gauge wrapper and skips tools already marked async. anyio.to_thread.run_sync was chosen over asyncio.to_thread specifically because it copies the caller's contextvars into the worker thread, which the op-cert signer binding depends on.\n\nSo the assumption this ticket was filed to flag as false is now true. What remains is not the original defect but a coverage question: is the drain contract PINNED to that property, so a future change that moved bodies back onto the loop would fail a test rather than silently re-break the contract? If nothing asserts it, that is worth keeping open — but as a regression-guard ticket with different acceptance criteria, not as the defect described here.\n\n**Relevant safety fact, verified rather than assumed:** the MCP server's drain IS bounded. drain_then_exit (_mcp_health.py:328) takes grace_seconds and computes 'deadline = monotonic() + grace_seconds', with tests covering both the drains-in-time and never-drains cases. That matters right now because the review-bot suffered an outage today from precisely the UNBOUNDED shape: a docker compose recreate SIGTERMed it, uvicorn closed its listening socket at the start of graceful shutdown, and lifespan shutdown never returned because in-flight reviews kept running — so the container reported Up, the worker kept voting for another ~18 minutes, and nothing was listening on port 8000. Review dispatch was dead for 22 minutes and, because the Gerrit webhooks plugin is not at-least-once (ADR 0009), events in that window were DROPPED rather than retried. Filed as unoutlawed-eloquent-amphibian (9d40-1db8-1bcc-4f91, P1).\n\nThe MCP server's bounded drain is what prevents that same failure there, which is a good argument for pinning it with a test rather than leaving it as an implementation detail.\n\nRecommend: re-scope this ticket to 'pin the off-loop execution property and the bounded drain with regression tests', or close it as obsolete and file that guard separately. Not doing either myself — the ticket is open and unclaimed, and re-scoping should be a deliberate choice."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787961058247159001,"uuid":"c9a4654b-b59b-4c83-a80d-276565ecf8e5"}